<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chichimeca War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1550–<a href="/years/1590">1590</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimeca</a> War was a long conflict between Spanish colonists and the nomadic and semi-nomadic peoples of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> known collectively as Chichimecas. Sparked by the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines, it centred on raids along the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> and the mines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ended it around 1590 less by force than by a policy of food and goods, settlements of allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a> and missions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chichimeca-war.jpg" alt="Chichimeca War" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Carlos Fonseca Mata · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_de_San_Miguel_y_San_Felipe_de_los_Chichimecas_(1580)_-_Chichimecas_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 8 September 1546 natives near the Cerro de la Bufa showed the Spaniard Juan de Tolosa silver-rich ore, and Spaniards soon crowded into what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>, followed by mining towns such as Fresnillo, Sombrerete and Mazapil. Spanish soldiers raided for slaves for the mines, and the new roads from Querétaro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a> carried caravans across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimeca</a> land. The war began eight years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixton-war">Mixtón War</a> of 1540–1542, and is sometimes seen as its continuation. The Chichimecas, perhaps 30,000 to 60,000 people, lived mostly by hunting and gathering; their four main nations were the Guachichiles, Pames, Guamares and Zacatecos, skilled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> whose obsidian-tipped arrows could pierce Spanish mail.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Fighting began in late 1550, when Zacatecos attacked supply routes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> and then Spanish settlements south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>. In 1551 the Guachichiles and Guamares killed 15 soldiers at San Miguel de Allende and forced its abandonment. Wagon trains were destroyed in 1553 and 1554 with goods worth 32,000 and 40,000 pesos, and by the end of 1561 more than 4,000 Spaniards and native allies were estimated to have been killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimecas</a> raided in groups of 40 to 50 warriors, learned to fight on horseback and used spies and lookouts.</p><p>In 1567 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> declared a war of fire and blood, promising death, enslavement or mutilation. It built a dozen presidios, beginning at San Miguel and San Felipe in 1562 and Nombre de Dios in 1563, and settled towns that grew into Celaya, León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aguascalientes">Aguascalientes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>, with Caxcan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a> allies. Yet attacks grew from 1575 to 1585, and in 1581 the viceroy, Conde de Coruna, wrote to Felipe II that many mines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> had closed. Soldiers' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> kept the hostility alive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1574 the Dominicans declared the war unjust and caused by Spanish aggression. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Guadalajara proposed new towns with priests in 1584, and from 1586 the viceroy Alvaro Manrique de Zuniga withdrew soldiers and offered tools, food, clothing and land, with the help of Miguel Caldera, a captain of Spanish and Guachichile descent. In 1591 Luis de Velasco persuaded 400 Tlaxcalan families to found eight settlements, in return for land, freedom from taxes and the right to bear arms. This purchase for peace shaped Spanish frontier policy for nearly three centuries. The Wixárika are believed to descend from the Guachichiles, and Pame and Chichimeca Jonaz speakers live on.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chichimeca War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The discovery of silver in the north provoked the <b>Chichimeca War</b>, a long conflict along the road from Mexico City to Zacatecas from about 1550 to 1590, which the Spaniards ended less by force than by a policy of gifts, settlement and missions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Military</a></li></ul></section>
